---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import crashes from "../assets/crashes.png";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
	<div class="p-4 flex items-center justify-center">
		<h1 class="text-4xl bg-ctp-mantle p-8 rounded-2xl">Burnout</h1>
	</div>
	<div class="flex flex-row flex-1 items-center justify-center flex-wrap gap-16 bg-ctp-mantle m-8 p-8 rounded-2xl">
		<div class="w-lg h-auto">
			<Image
				class="object-contain inline"
				src={crashes}
				alt="Photo by Nataliya Vaitkevich from Pexels: https://www.pexels.com/photo/overworked-employee-lying-in-front-of-laptop-6837643/"
			/>
		</div>
		<p class="text-xl">
			Commuting to and from work not only takes time, but contributes to a large portion of the fatal crashes on the road. Out of all the states in the US, the
			lowest portion of fatal crashes that were during commute hours was in Connecticut, with 20.8% of their fatal crashes taking place during commute hours.
			That number climbs up to 29.6% in New Hampshire (Fatal Commuter Crashes). A well-implemented shortened workday could greatly improve these statistics. If
			the workday was shorter, the shifts scheduled by different companies could be shuffled around, to lessen the spikes in traffic at certain times. This
			would also lead to shorter commutes, as there would be fewer drivers on the road. With cars being a massive contributor to carbon emissions and pollution
			(Car Harm), lowering the amount of time each car spends on the road would lead to a strong improvement in the amount of pollution being produced,
			especially if implemented on a scale as large as the US. While public transit is an option in some areas, the systems are often exclusive to more urban
			areas, with those in rural or suburban areas needing a car to get to and from work, if they can afford it (Transit Investment). In those urban areas, the
			benefit would still be seen from more spread-out shifts, as trans cars and buses wouldn’t be crammed full, like they often are with the standard 8 hour
			workday. While there were spikes in fatalities for both the morning and evening commute times, the evening commute times’ spike was much greater. This may
			be partially attributed to drowsy driving, which led to 91,000 police reports and nearly 800 deaths in 2017 (Drowsy Driving). Drowsy driving isn’t
			mentioned as often as driving under the influence, although the two have similar effects. After being awake for 17 hours , the level of impairment was
			seen to be similar if not “equivalent to having a blood alcohol content (BAC) of .05. After 24 hours awake, impairment is equivalent to a BAC of .10”
			(Driver Fatigue). While the 8 hour workday isn’t anywhere near 17 hours, even if the longest transport time and plenty of preparation in the morning are
			assumed, the equivalence doesn’t take into account the degree of work done in those 17 hours. More mentally or physically taxing work may have a stronger
			impact on the level of impairment, meaning that it’s possible for an 8 hour workday to cause enough impairment to adversely affect driving skills. A
			shorter workday, even if the intensity of the work done is the same, would thus be less exhausting to the workers, leaving them in a safer state for the
			possibly long drive home. Lessened traffic and more alert workers would make for much safer roads, and even improve the quality of public transportation
			in places where it’s present.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div class="flex items-center justify-center">
		<a href="/" class="underline text-2xl bg-ctp-mantle p-4 rounded-2xl">back</a>
	</div>
	<div class="flex items-center justify-center">
		<a href="/smog" class="underline text-2xl bg-ctp-mantle p-4 rounded-2xl">next</a>
	</div>
</Layout>
